WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.530
If you've ever played the Super Nintendo classic
Super Mario World, you've seen it.

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:09.001
As Yoshi and Mario gallivant through each
level, Yoshi pulls a Miley Cyrus circa 2015

00:00:09.001 --> 00:00:13.400
to grab certain items with his tongue, at
Mario's command.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.900
While some players argued that their favorite
mustachioed plumber was simply pointing at

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:22.090
what he wanted Yoshi to slurp up, others had
a more sinister suggestion: Mario was actually

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:24.050
punching Yoshi in the head.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:28.289
Believe it or not, we've since discovered
that Team Head Punch was absolutely right.

00:00:28.289 --> 00:00:33.559
In an interview on Nintendo's official site
in September 2017, Super Mario World developers

00:00:33.559 --> 00:00:38.850
Takashi Tezuka and Shigefumi Hino discussed
how the character of Yoshi evolved, including

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:41.719
his much buzzed-about tongue animation.

00:00:41.719 --> 00:00:45.269
Hino said, "Lots of people think that while
Mario is pointing his finger forward, he's

00:00:45.269 --> 00:00:48.280
saying 'Go,' and Yoshi's tongue comes out.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:52.910
However, the setup that I drew was that when
Mario punches Yoshi in the head, the character's

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:55.219
tongue shoots out in surprise."

00:00:55.219 --> 00:00:59.280
But the developers didn't want gamers to think
Mario was mean, so the official explanation

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:04.350
became that Mario was merely gesturing at
what he wanted his dino pal to retrieve.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:07.369
Official game art was drawn up to reflect
this as well, but there was no change to the

00:01:07.369 --> 00:01:12.219
actual animation in the game, which was low-res
enough to obscure the Yoshi-abuse.

00:01:12.219 --> 00:01:16.560
Hino also said there used to be a "bop" sound
to accompany the punch, but, "it seemed like

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.939
people would say 'Poor Yoshi,'" so they changed
it to this sound …

00:01:19.939 --> 00:01:23.979
… which Hino says is supposed to represent
Mario saying "Go!"

00:01:23.979 --> 00:01:29.270
So now you know — the in-game animation
was, indeed, intended to depict Mario punching

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:33.509
his trusty dino-steed into submission, but
Nintendo covered up the mistreatment with

00:01:33.509 --> 00:01:36.579
a sweeter sound effect and some very effective
propaganda.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:42.759
"That'll teach you to mess with my new little
buddy Yoshi!"

00:01:42.759 --> 00:01:43.759
Thanks for watching!

